    <!-- =========================================================
         INTRODUCTION
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column opening">

      <p class="lead">
        Washington Irving’s connection with homeopathy does not depend upon
        a quotation attributed to him generations later. His own letters
        survive—and so does the medical account of the physician who treated him.
      </p>

      <p>
        Few names are more closely associated with early American literature.
        Irving’s
        <em> Sketch Book of Geoffrey Crayon, Gent.</em>,
        published in 1819–1820, introduced readers on both sides of the Atlantic
        to stories that would become enduring pieces of American culture.
      </p>

      <p>
        But Irving’s career extended well beyond fiction. He wrote substantial
        historical and biographical works, including books about Christopher
        Columbus and George Washington, traveled extensively in Europe, and
        represented the United States abroad. From 1842 to 1846, Irving served
        as United States Minister to Spain.
      </p>

      <p>
        During the final decade of his life, Irving also received homeopathic medical care.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rather than relying upon later accounts of Irving’s relationship with
        homeopathy, I returned to two nineteenth-century sources:
        <strong> Irving’s published correspondence </strong>
        and the medical account written by his treating physician,
        <strong> Dr. John C. Peters</strong>.
        Read together, they allow the story to be reconstructed in remarkable detail.
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         PETERS / BEGINNING OF CARE
