        <div class="reading-column">

          <p class="section-label">
            The Physician’s Account
          </p>

          <h2>
            A Medical Relationship <br /> That Lasted More Than <br />Seven Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            By the early 1850s, Irving was approaching seventy and living at
            Sunnyside, his beloved home overlooking the Hudson River near
            Tarrytown, New York.
          </p>

          <p>
            He was still intensely engaged in literary work and had begun one
            of the largest projects of his career: the multivolume biography
            that would become his
            <em> Life of George Washington</em>.
          </p>

          <p>
            In February 1852, Irving and his family came under the care of
            Dr. John C. Peters, a New York physician closely associated with
            nineteenth-century homeopathy and later an editor of the
            <em> North American Journal of Homoeopathy</em>.
          </p>

          <p>
            After Irving’s death, Peters published his own account,
            <em> The Illnesses of Washington Irving</em>.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="inline-quote">

            <p>
              “I commenced to treat Mr. Irving and his family in Feb., 1852…”
            </p>

          </blockquote>

          <p>
            Dr. Peters reported that during the years that followed he made
            <strong> 236 trips to Sunnyside</strong>,
            paid
            <strong> 266 professional visits </strong>
            to the family in town, and received
            <strong> 96 calls </strong>
            from them at his own residence.
          </p>

          <p>
            At times, Peters remained at Sunnyside all day. Frequently, he
            stayed through the night. On some occasions he remained from
            Saturday afternoon until Monday morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            His last personal interview with Irving occurred on
            <strong> November 15, 1859</strong>.
            His last prescription was sent through Irving’s nephew,
            Pierre M. Irving, on
            <strong> November 25</strong>.
            Although Irving had endured a prolonged and complicated illness, his final months brought a remarkable period of relief. 
            His asthma and cough subsided, his strength and appetite improved, and his nights became less troubled. 
            Less than six weeks before his death, he told his nephew Pierre, “I feel that I am growing stronger and better.” 
            Irving died suddenly on November 28, 1859. In a letter written afterward, Pierre thanked Dr. Peters for his devoted care and attributed his uncle’s 
            “partial improvement and comparative comfort” during those final months to Dr. Peters’s treatment.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
