            1852
          </p>

          <h2>
            Vertigo, an Overtaxed Mind—and Cocculus
          </h2>

          <p>
            Peters tells us what was happening when he first became Irving’s
            physician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Irving was working on the first volume of his
            <em> Life of Washington </em>
            when he began suffering from
            <strong> vertigo</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peters described Irving as very healthy for someone in his late 60's,
            but wrote that he had
            <strong> “latterly begun to be troubled with vertigo.” </strong>
          </p>

          <p>
            The symptoms appeared to mimic stroke but Dr. Peters realized the intense work Irving was doing 
            caused an <strong> “overtaxed condition of his brain” </strong> instead of 
            his health actually declining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peters then identified the remedy and the result he observed.
          </p>


          <blockquote class="pull-quote">

            <p>
              “The dizziness was removed by the persistent and systematic
              use of Cocculus…”
            </p>

          </blockquote>


          <p>
            Peters also credited Irving’s disciplined lifestyle with helping his recovery.
          </p>

          <p>
            The record is unusually specific: we know the physician,
            the complaint, the remedy, and the outcome Peters reported.
          </p>

          <p>
            But the story becomes even more interesting when Peters’s account
            is placed beside Washington Irving’s own private correspondence.
          </p>

        </div>


