        <div class="reading-column">

          <p class="section-label">
            Irving in His Own Words
          </p>

          <h2>
            “I Have Found, in My Own Case, Great Relief from Homoeopathy”
          </h2>

          <p>
            On February 21, 1854, Irving wrote from Sunnyside to his friend
            Mrs. Kennedy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Her husband had an illness affecting his head.
            Irving responded by describing his own experience.
          </p>


          <blockquote class="major-quote">

            <p>
              “I have found, in my own case, great relief from homoeopathy,
              to which I had recourse almost accidentally, for I am rather
              slow at adopting new theories.”
            </p>

          </blockquote>


          <p>
            This final admission gives the passage much of its historical
            significance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Irving did not portray himself as someone eager to embrace a new
            medical system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quite the opposite.
          </p>

          <p>
            He was, in his own words,
            <strong> “rather slow at adopting new theories.” </strong>
          </p>

          <p>
            Yet his personal experience had apparently challenged his caution.
          </p>


          <blockquote class="major-quote emphasis">

            <p>
              “I can now apply myself to literary occupation day after day
              for several hours at a time, without any recurrence of the
              symptoms that troubled me.”
            </p>

          </blockquote>


          <p>
            For Irving, that was no small measure of improvement.
            Writing was one of the central activities of his life, and the
            symptoms had interfered with his ability to do so.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because of homeopathy, he could now return to what he had spent a lifetime doing: writing.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         EVIDENCE / CAUTION
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column comparison-section">

      <p class="section-label">
        Reading the Sources Carefully
      </p>

      <h2>
        Were Irving and Peters <br />Describing the Same Complaint?
      </h2>

      <p>
        Peters’s medical account and Irving’s private correspondence fit
        together remarkably well, but historical sources should not be made
        to say more than they actually do.
      </p>

      <p>
        Peters describes
        <strong> vertigo </strong>
        present when he assumed Irving’s care and reports treating the
        dizziness with Cocculus.
      </p>

      <p>
        Irving’s letters from 1854 instead call his problem an
        <strong> “affection of the head” </strong>
        and a
        <strong> “complaint of the head.” </strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        In his August letter, Irving specifically says that this complaint
        had troubled him during the previous year—1853.
      </p>


      <aside class="evidence-note">

        <h3>
          What the Record Allows Us to Say
        </h3>

        <p>
          Neither Irving nor Peters explicitly states that Irving’s later
          “complaint of the head” was identical to the vertigo Peters
          described when he first assumed Irving’s care.
        </p>

        <p>
          It may have represented the same condition, a recurrence of it,
          or a closely related complaint.
        </p>

        <p>
          What can be established independently is that Peters reported
          treating Irving’s vertigo with Cocculus and that Irving himself
          later documented substantial relief from homeopathy for a head
          complaint serious enough to interfere with his literary work.
        </p>

      </aside>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         AUGUST 1854 LETTER
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="feature-band soft">

      <div class="side-layout two-column-right">

        <div class="reading-column">

          <p class="section-label">
            Six Months Later
          </p>

          <h2>
            “I Really Begin to Have Great Faith in Them”
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fortunately, Irving’s correspondence allows us to follow the
            story beyond a single report of improvement.
          </p>

          <p>
            On August 31, 1854, Mrs. Kennedy asked Irving if he was still using homeopathy.
          </p>

          <p>
            His answer was considerably more enthusiastic.
          </p>


          <blockquote class="pull-quote">

            <p>
              “I really begin to have great faith in them.”
            </p>


          </blockquote>


          <p>
            Irving then returned specifically to the complaint that had
            interfered with his writing.
          </p>


          <blockquote class="pull-quote second">

            <p>
              “The complaint of the head especially, which troubled me last
              year, and obliged me to throw by my pen, has been completely
              vanquished by them…”
            </p>

          </blockquote>


          <p>
            The progression preserved in these two letters is striking.
          </p>

          <div class="progression">

            <p>
              <strong> February 1854:</strong><br />
              I was slow to adopt new theories, but I have found great relief
              from homeopathy.
            </p>

            <p>
              <strong> August 1854:</strong><br />
              I really begin to have great faith in them.
            </p>

          </div>



          <p>
            <strong>
              We can actually watch Irving’s opinion change through his own
              correspondence.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <figure class="side-image side-right letter-scan">

