            Washington Irving, August 31, 1854.
            <em> The Life and Letters of Washington Irving</em>,
            Vol. IV, p. 179.
          </figcaption>

        </figure>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         1857
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column relationship-section">

      <p class="section-label">
        A Relationship That Continued
      </p>

      <h2>
  “Those Powders Which<br />
  Proved So Efficacious Before”
</h2>

      <p>
        Irving’s relationship with Peters did not end with the letters from
        1854.
      </p>

      <p>
        Peters’s medical account reproduces several letters he received
        directly from Irving.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before Peters departed for Europe in June 1857, Irving wrote:
      </p>


      <blockquote class="quote-card">

        <p>
          “I wish, before you embark on your short trip to Europe, you will
          have put up for me a few of those powders which proved so efficacious
          before.”
        </p>

        <footer>
          — Washington Irving to Dr. John C. Peters,
          June 22, 1857
        </footer>

      </blockquote>


      <p>
        Irving then invited Peters to Sunnyside and jokingly told him that he
        expected Dr. Peters to make everyone in his house so healthy that they would not have 
        any illnesses while he was away.
      </p>

      <p>
        Peters printed this correspondence while discussing another illness
        for which he had treated Irving: a longstanding eruptive irritation
        around the ankles.
      </p>

      <p>
        Peters reported treating the condition not with salves but through the
        <strong> “somewhat persistent use of Hepar-sulphur,” </strong>
        after which he said Irving recovered from it beautifully for the rest of his life.
      </p>

      <p>
        The surviving 1857 letter itself does not identify what medicine was
        contained in the “powders.” We therefore should not assume they were
        necessarily Hepar sulphur—or Cocculus, but homeopathaic remedies are often given in a powder form even today.
      </p>

      <p>
        Irving’s statement requires no embellishment.
      </p>

      <p>
        He was requesting medicines from Peters because earlier powders the
        physician had supplied had, in Irving’s own words,
        <strong> “proved so efficacious before.” </strong>
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         CONFIDENCE IN PETERS
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="feature-band confidence-section">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          Physician and Patient
        </p>

        <h2>
          Irving’s Confidence in Dr. Peters
        </h2>

        <p>
          The surviving record also reveals how much confidence Irving came
          to place in his physician.
        </p>

        <p>
          Shortly before beginning
          <em> The Illnesses of Washington Irving</em>,
          Peters wrote that Irving had developed the habit of
          <strong> “always relying solely upon me for medical advice,” </strong>
          despite more than twenty miles separating them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Peters reproduced another note from Irving in which the writer asked
          him to attend a visiting friend suffering from a severe cold and the
          man’s daughter, who had a sore throat.
        </p>

        <p>
          Irving playfully asked Peters to
          <strong> “make them both well, as I told them you would do immediately.” </strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          The wording should not be read as a literal guarantee of medical
          success. But as personal correspondence, it illustrates the
          familiarity and confidence that had developed between physician
          and patient.
        </p>

        <p>
          Peters eventually referred to Irving as his
          <strong> “kind patron, friend, and patient.” </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         FROM CAUTION TO CONFIDENCE
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="article-section reading-column">

      <p class="section-label">
        Historical Perspective
      </p>

      <h2>
        From Caution to Confidence
      </h2>

      <p>
        Washington Irving’s story is valuable precisely because it does not
        need embellishment.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is no evidence that Irving became a homeopath,
        studied homeopathy, or became a public spokesman
        for the profession.
      </p>

      <p>
        The historical evidence tells a simpler—and arguably more
        compelling—story.
      </p>


      <div class="timeline-card">

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span class="timeline-year">1852</span>
          <p>
            Peters records treating Irving’s vertigo with
            <strong> Cocculus </strong>
            and reports that the dizziness was removed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span class="timeline-year">Feb. 1854</span>
          <p>
            Irving reports
            <strong> “great relief from homoeopathy” </strong>
            and says he can again work for hours without recurrence of the
            symptoms that troubled him.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span class="timeline-year">Aug. 1854</span>
          <p>
            Irving says,
            <strong> “I really begin to have great faith in them,” </strong>
            and describes his head complaint as
            <strong> “completely vanquished.” </strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span class="timeline-year">1857</span>
          <p>
            Irving asks Peters for additional powders because previous ones
            had
            <strong> “proved so efficacious before.” </strong>
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>


      <p>
        That is not evidence of a passing curiosity.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is evidence of a patient’s developing confidence based upon what
        <strong> he had experienced</strong>.
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================================================
         TWO SIDES OF THE HISTORICAL RECORD
         ========================================================= -->

    <section class="primary-source record-section">

      <div class="reading-column">

        <p class="section-label">
          Two Sides of the Historical Record
        </p>

        <h2>
          The Patient’s Testimony and the Physician’s Account
        </h2>

        <p>
          Washington Irving belongs among the stronger documentary examples
          in the
          <em> Homeopathy in History </em>
          collection because his story can be reconstructed from two
          complementary nineteenth-century sources.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first preserves
          <strong> Irving’s own correspondence</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
          His nephew and biographer,
          <strong> Pierre M. Irving</strong>,
          published
          <em> The Life and Letters of Washington Irving</em>.
          Volume IV, published by G. P. Putnam in New York in 1864, contains
          both of Irving’s 1854 letters to Mrs. Kennedy.
        </p>

        <div class="record-quotes">

          <blockquote class="major-quote">
            <p>
              “I have found, in my own case, great relief from homoeopathy…”
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="major-quote emphasis">
            <p>
              “I really begin to have great faith in them.”
            </p>
          </blockquote>

        </div>

        <p>
          These are not statements attributed to Irving by a twentieth- or
          twenty-first-century writer.
        </p>

        <p>
          They are Irving’s correspondence, published only a few years after
          his death.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second source gives us the
          <strong> physician’s side of the story</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 1860, John C. Peters published {" "}
          <em> 
            A Review of Some of the Late Reforms in Pathology and Therapeutics:
            With an Appendix on the Illnesses of Washington Irving
          </em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          There Peters records beginning his treatment of Irving and his family
          in February 1852, describes Irving’s vertigo, identifies
          <strong> Cocculus</strong>,
          and reports that
          <strong> “the dizziness was removed.” </strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Peters’s account appeared in 1860, only months after Irving’s death
          on November 28, 1859.
        </p>

        <p class="record-closing">
          Together, these sources give us something unusually valuable:
          <strong>
            {" "} the patient’s testimony and the treating physician’s account.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


