    <section class="feature-band lasting-philosophy">

      <div class="lasting-inner">

        <p class="section-label">
          Why Washington Irving’s Story Matters
        </p>

        <h2>
          His Experience, Preserved in His Own Words
        </h2>

        <div class="lasting-copy">

          <p>
            Irving lived through an extraordinary period in American medicine.
            Homeopathy arrived in the United States during his lifetime and
            expanded rapidly during the nineteenth century, developing
            physicians, pharmacies, professional societies, medical colleges,
            and hospitals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Irving’s correspondence allows us to glimpse that medical world
            not through institutional statistics or professional arguments,
            but through the experience of an individual patient.
          </p>

          <p>
            And that patient happened to be one of the foundational figures
            of American literature.
          </p>

          <p>
            More than three decades before these events, Irving’s imagination
            had given American culture
            <em> Rip Van Winkle </em>
            and
            <em> The Legend of Sleepy Hollow</em>.
          </p>

          <p>
            By the time John C. Peters became his physician in 1852, Irving
            was an older man working on an entirely different kind of legacy:
            his monumental biography of George Washington.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="lasting-divider"></div>


        <div class="lasting-closing">

          <p class="closing-line">
            He did not accept it immediately.<br />
            He tried it.<br />
            He observed what happened.<br />
            And then he wrote about the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Perhaps no sentence captures Washington Irving’s place in the
            history of homeopathy better than the one he wrote privately from
            Sunnyside on February 21, 1854:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="closing-quote">

            <p>
              “I have found, in my own case, great relief from homoeopathy…”
            </p>

          </blockquote>

          <p class="final-lines">
            Not legend.<br />
            Not a quotation attributed to him generations later.<br />
            <strong>
              His experience, preserved in his own correspondence—and
              accompanied by the surviving account of the physician who
              treated him.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


